From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CeraLive <dev@ceralive.tv>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 15:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 23/25] media: rockchip: rkvenc: stop reading a task the worker has already released

MOTIVATION. A KASAN+lockdep Rock 5B+ running
tests/rkvenc-fault-qa.sh --case fail-clock-enable reported 'BUG:
KASAN: use-after-free in rkvenc_task_worker_default+0xcfc/0x10dc,
Read of size 8 at addr ffff00010da88030', shadow all 0xff and the
page refcount 0 -- long freed, not freshly poisoned.
rkvenc_task_finish() ends in kref_put(&task->ref,
rkvenc_free_task_callback), which kfree()s the whole struct
rkvenc_task. A task carries exactly two references, the
allocator's and the one rkvenc_dev_ioctl() takes for the waiter,
so whichever of the worker and the waiter puts last is the one
that frees -- and the worker keeps using the pointer either way.
Its IRQ arm calls rkvenc_task_finish() and the timeout arm
immediately below re-reads mpp_task->state; state sits at offset
0x30 of the allocation behind three list_heads, test_bit() is an
8-byte instrumented read, and sizeof(struct rkvenc_task) is 11408
bytes, so the object comes from the page allocator page-aligned --
which is the address, the width and the page-not-slab report
KASAN printed, to the byte. Its hw_run-failure arm calls
rkvenc_task_finish() and then wake_up(&pending_task->wait), the
same defect one field along.

BEHAVIOUR. The worker stops touching a task it has handed to
rkvenc_task_finish(). The IRQ arm nulls its local afterwards, so
the timeout arm cannot read the freed state word. No reachable
work is skipped: rkvenc_hw_irq() and rkvenc_task_timeout_work()
both claim a task with test_and_set_bit(TASK_STATE_HANDLE), and
the timeout side does so with the encoder's IRQ disabled, so
TASK_STATE_IRQ and TASK_STATE_TIMEOUT are never both set on one
task -- and had that gate ever broken, the old code would have
called rkvenc_task_finish() twice on it and put one reference
twice. The hw_run-failure arm loses its trailing wake_up():
rkvenc_task_finish() sets TASK_STATE_DONE and wakes task->wait on
every path, including 0021's teardown-skipping one, so the waiter
is already awake and the second wake only read a freed wait queue.

NON-GOALS. Does not change the reference-counting model: two
references, two owners and three drop sites, exactly as 0001 and
0014 left them, and no kref_get() is added to hold the window open
instead of closing it. Does not touch 0021 -- its
TASK_STATE_HW_HELD bookkeeping is correct and hardware-confirmed,
and reverting it would trade this bug for the rwsem and
runtime-PM imbalance it fixed. Does not touch rkvenc_task_finish()
itself, 0014's quiesce/drain, or 0020's per-core requiesce. No new
lock, no new wait, no fault-injection knob.

PROVENANCE. First-party CeraLive fix. Both dereferences are 0001's
as imported and no patch between them changes either: 0013 inserts
its delay BEFORE the IRQ arm's finish, 0019 changes only the queue
lock types, 0021 adds one atomic_inc() before the failure arm's
finish and jumps into the same unconditional kref_put() tail. What
0021 changed is reachability -- before it this path double-released
the reset rwsem and the PM references, the encode wedged, and the
worker never ran on far enough to be caught here. No upstream
counterpart exists.

EVIDENCE POINTER. Root-caused from a REAL Rock 5B+ KASAN report,
then pinned to the line on the applied v7.1.7 tree: pahole on a
KASAN+PROVE_LOCKING arm64 build of this exact series puts
rkvenc_mpp_task.state at offset 48 size 8 and struct rkvenc_task
at 11408 bytes, and 0xffff00010da88030 is 0x30 into a 16 KiB-
aligned allocation. wait is at offset 112, so the report names the
timeout arm's test_bit() and not the failure arm's wake_up(); both
are fixed. HOST-VERIFIED ONLY -- build-clean at W=1 and gate-clean,
with NO hardware re-run. The fault-QA harness already asserts the
correct behaviour, so no test expectation changes. See
docs/UPSTREAM-STATUS.md and docs/BOARD-QUALIFICATION.md.

First-party: authored by CeraLive against v7.1.7, with no upstream
counterpart in rockchip-rk3588-mainline-patches. The source of record is
ceralive/0023-rkvenc-worker-task-lifetime.patch; patches/ is generated from it by
scripts/build-series.py, and scripts/verify-payload-parity.py holds it to the
same added/removed-line parity the upstream lane gets.

NOT upstream-bound: this targets the CeraLive device tree only and is not a
submission to linux-media, linux-rockchip or the fork parent. No Signed-off-by
is added, because a DCO assertion belongs to whoever actually submits it.

---
diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/rkvenc/rkvenc_service.c
@@ -928,6 +928,18 @@ void rkvenc_task_worker_default(struct kthread_work *work)
 		rkvenc_task_finish(session, mpp_task);
 
 		set_bit(enc->core_id, &queue->core_idle);
+
+		/*
+		 * That call dropped the reference this core held, and the
+		 * waiter it woke on the way out may already have dropped the
+		 * other one -- in which case the task was kfree()d inside it.
+		 * The timeout arm below reads mpp_task->state, so the pointer
+		 * has to die HERE. Nothing reachable is lost: rkvenc_hw_irq()
+		 * and rkvenc_task_timeout_work() both claim the task with
+		 * test_and_set_bit(TASK_STATE_HANDLE), so TASK_STATE_IRQ and
+		 * TASK_STATE_TIMEOUT are never both set on one task.
+		 */
+		mpp_task = NULL;
 	}
 
 	/* Process timed-out tasks */
@@ -1007,9 +1019,17 @@ void rkvenc_task_worker_default(struct kthread_work *work)
 				atomic_inc(&pending_task->abort_request);
 				set_bit(TASK_STATE_DONE, &pending_task->state);
 				set_bit(target->core_id, &queue->core_idle);
+				/*
+				 * Sets TASK_STATE_DONE, wakes task->wait and
+				 * drops the reference this core held -- all
+				 * three on every path, including the one that
+				 * skips the hardware teardown -- so the task
+				 * may be freed by the time it returns. The
+				 * waiter is already awake and nothing below
+				 * may touch pending_task again.
+				 */
 				rkvenc_task_finish(pending_task->session,
 						  pending_task);
-				wake_up(&pending_task->wait);
 			}
 		}
 	}
